[171.455245, "o", "Chunk 87 (PASSAGE_0087): '8.6 Hepatic Impairment\\nThe daily recommended dose of celecoxib capsules in patients with moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class B) should be re'\r\nChunk 89 (PASSAGE_0089): '8.8 Poor Metabolizers of CYP2C9 Substrates\\nIn patients who are known or suspected to be poor CYP2C9 metabolizers (i.e., CYP2C9*3/*3), based on genotyp'\r\nChunk 90 (PASSAGE_0090): '10 OVERDOSAGE\\nSymptoms following acute NSAID overdosages have been typically limited to lethargy, drowsiness, nausea, vomiting, and epigastric pain, w'\r\nChunk 93 (PASSAGE_0093): '12.2 Pharmacodynamics\\nPlatelets\\nIn clinical trials using normal volunteers, celecoxib capsules at single doses up to 800 mg and multiple doses of 600 '\r\nChunk 95 (PASSAGE_0095): '12.3 Pharmacokinetics\\nCelecoxib exhibits dose-proportional increase in exposure after oral administration up to 200 mg twice daily and less than propo'\r\nChunk 96 (PASSAGE_0096): 'Absorption\\nPeak plasma levels of celecoxib occur approximately 3 hours after an oral dose. Under fasting conditions, both peak plasma levels (C\\nmax) a'\r\nChunk 97 (PASSAGE_0097): 'Food Effects\\nWhen celecoxib capsules were taken with a high fat meal, peak plasma levels were delayed for about 1 to 2 hours with an increase in total'\r\nChunk 98 (PASSAGE_0098): 'Distribution\\nIn healthy subjects, celecoxib is highly protein bound (~97%) within the clinical dose range. In vitro studies indicate that celecoxib bi'\r\nChunk 100 (PASSAGE_0100): 'Excretion\\nCelecoxib is eliminated predominantly by hepatic metabolism with little (<3%) unchanged drug recovered in the urine and feces. Following a s'\r\nChunk 101 (PASSAGE_0101): 'Geriatric\\nAt steady state, elderly subjects (over 65 years old) had a 40% higher C\\nmaxand a 50% higher AUC compared to the young subjects. In elderly '\r\nChunk 104 (PASSAGE_0104): 'Hepatic Impairment\\nA pharmacokinetic study in subjects with mild (Child-Pugh Class A) and moderate (Child-Pugh Class B) hepatic impairment has shown t'\r\n"]
[171.455328, "o", "Chunk 112 (PASSAGE_0112): '13.1 Carcinogenesis, Mutagenesis, Impairment of Fertility\\nCarcinogenesis\\nCelecoxib was not carcinogenic in Sprague-Dawley rats given oral doses up to '\r\nChunk 114 (PASSAGE_0114): 'Impairment of Fertility\\nCelecoxib had no effect on male or female fertility or male reproductive function in rats at oral doses up to 600 mg/kg/day (a'\r\nChunk 115 (PASSAGE_0115): '13.2 Animal Toxicology\\nAn increase in the incidence of background findings of spermatocele with or without secondary changes such as epididymal hyposp'\r\n"]
[171.455349, "o", "Chunk 116 (PASSAGE_0116): '14.1 Osteoarthritis\\nCelecoxib capsules have demonstrated significant reduction in joint pain compared to placebo. Celecoxib capsules were evaluated fo'\r\nChunk 117 (PASSAGE_0117): '14.2 Rheumatoid Arthritis\\nCelecoxib capsules have demonstrated significant reduction in joint tenderness/pain and joint swelling compared to placebo. '\r\nChunk 119 (PASSAGE_0119): '14.4 Ankylosing Spondylitis\\nCelecoxib capsules were evaluated in AS patients in two placebo- and active-controlled clinical trials of 6 and 12 weeks d'\r\nChunk 120 (PASSAGE_0120): '14.5 Analgesia, Including Primary Dysmenorrhea\\nIn acute analgesic models of post-oral surgery pain, post-orthopedic surgical pain, and primary dysmeno'\r\nChunk 122 (PASSAGE_0122): 'Design\\nThe PRECISION trial was a double-blind randomized controlled trial of cardiovascular safety in OA and RA patients with or at high risk for card'\r\nChunk 123 (PASSAGE_0123): 'Results\\nAmong subjects with OA, only 0.2% (17/7259) escalated celecoxib to the 200 mg twice daily dose, whereas 54.7% (3946/7208) escalated ibuprofen '\r\n"]
[171.455395, "o", "Chunk 124 (PASSAGE_0124): 'Primary Endpoint\\nThe trial had two prespecified analysis populations:\\n- Intent-to-treat population (ITT): Comprised of all randomized subjects followe'\r\nChunk 129 (PASSAGE_0129): 'Ambulatory Blood Pressure Monitoring (ABPM) Substudy\\nIn the PRECISION-ABPM substudy, among the total of 444 analyzable patients at Month 4, celecoxib '\r\n"]
